    {
      "id": "4ea04fe8-5807-4f98-9fd4-02e2a4294d93",
      "name": "Why MCP is a Game-Changer for AI Development",
      "path": "WWW / Blog / Why MCP is a Game-Changer for AI Development",
      "docTypeId": "086d156c-e06e-491c-85c0-788abd2f38d9",
      "templateId": "45cbafe1-1e54-4d24-bf71-feb52cb887d3",
      "state": "Published",
      "domains": [],
      "values": [
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "pageTitle",
          "value": "Why MCP is a Game-Changer for AI Development"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "excerpt",
          "value": "The Model Context Protocol (MCP) is transforming how AI agents interact with tools and services. Here's why developers should care."
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "featuredImage",
          "value": [
            {
              "key": "c4444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444",
              "mediaKey": "021f7bf3-beb7-48d3-b1a2-f1b38231799f",
              "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
              "crops": [],
              "focalPoint": null
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "author",
          "value": "AI Dev Team"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.DateTime",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "publishDate",
          "value": "2026-10-09 09:00:00"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "bodyText",
          "value": {
            "markup": "<h2>What is MCP?</h2><p>The Model Context Protocol (MCP) is an open standard that defines how AI applications connect to external tools and data sources. Think of it as USB-C for AI — a universal connector that lets language models talk to databases, APIs, file systems, and more.</p><h2>Why It Matters</h2><p>Before MCP, every AI integration was a custom affair. You'd write bespoke code to connect a model to your data, maintain it yourself, and rewrite it for every new model or tool. MCP changes this by providing:</p><ul><li><strong>Standardization</strong> — One protocol works across models and tools</li><li><strong>Discoverability</strong> — Clients can ask servers what capabilities they offer</li><li><strong>Security</strong> — Fine-grained permissions for what tools can access</li><li><strong>Simplicity</strong> — A server can expose dozens of tools with minimal code</li></ul><h2>Real-World Impact</h2><p>Teams using MCP report dramatically faster integration times. What used to take weeks of custom engineering now takes hours. And because the protocol is open, the ecosystem grows every day with new servers for databases, analytics platforms, and cloud services.</p>",
            "blocks": {
              "layout": {},
              "contentData": [],
              "settingsData": [],
              "expose": []
            }
          }
        }
      ],
      "urls": [
        "/why-mcp-is-a-game-changer-for-ai-development/"
      ],
      "docType": "blogPost",
      "template": "blogPost"
    },
    {
      "id": "cf05a73b-8801-4d4a-b5e8-c22918db1fd6",
      "name": "How MCP Connects AI to the Real World",
      "path": "WWW / Blog / How MCP Connects AI to the Real World",
      "docTypeId": "086d156c-e06e-491c-85c0-788abd2f38d9",
      "templateId": "45cbafe1-1e54-4d24-bf71-feb52cb887d3",
      "state": "Published",
      "domains": [],
      "values": [
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "pageTitle",
          "value": "How MCP Connects AI to the Real World"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextArea",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "excerpt",
          "value": "From databases to APIs to content management systems — MCP bridges the gap between AI models and the systems they need to interact with."
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.MediaPicker3",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "featuredImage",
          "value": [
            {
              "key": "d4444444-4444-4444-4444-444444444444",
              "mediaKey": "d402b379-48cf-467c-8d3e-ad9ec08a19c6",
              "mediaTypeAlias": "Image",
              "crops": [],
              "focalPoint": null
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.TextBox",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "author",
          "value": "AI Dev Team"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.DateTime",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "publishDate",
          "value": "2026-10-09 10:00:00"
        },
        {
          "editorAlias": "Umbraco.RichText",
          "culture": null,
          "segment": null,
          "alias": "bodyText",
          "value": {
            "markup": "<h2>Beyond the Chat Interface</h2><p>AI models are incredibly powerful, but they've traditionally been stuck inside a chat window. To do real work — query a database, send an email, update a CMS — they need connections to the outside world. That's exactly what MCP provides.</p><h2>How MCP Works</h2><p>MCP follows a client-server architecture:</p><ul><li><strong>MCP Clients</strong> — AI applications like Claude Code, IDE extensions, or custom tools that need to interact with external systems</li><li><strong>MCP Servers</strong> — Lightweight servers that expose tools and resources. Each server connects to one backend system (a database, a file system, an API)</li><li><strong>The Protocol</strong> — A standardized JSON-RPC-based communication layer that handles tool discovery, invocation, and resource access</li></ul><h2>MCP + Umbraco: A Real Example</h2><p>Take this very blog post. It was created through an MCP server connected to Umbraco — the AI agent queried the CMS, created content types, uploaded media, and published pages, all through a standardized protocol. No custom API wrappers, no brittle screen-scraping — just clean, typed tool calls mapped to Umbraco's Management API.</p><p>That's the promise of MCP: any AI tool can talk to any system, as long as both speak the same protocol.</p>",
            "blocks": {
              "layout": {},
              "contentData": [],
              "settingsData": [],
              "expose": []
            }
          }
        }
      ],
      "urls": [
        "/how-mcp-connects-ai-to-the-real-world/"
      ],
      "docType": "blogPost",
      "template": "blogPost"
    }
